WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.567
Qdrant.

2
00:00:01.867 --> 00:00:06.400
The production-grade, self-hosted vector database, built around filtering.

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.233
Qdrant is a Rust server.

4
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:14.600
Run it as one Docker container, or as a cluster, over gRPC and REST.

5
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.500
It supports hybrid search and snapshots, and is known for predictable performance.

6
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.500
Filtering is the key difference.

7
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.567
Filtering after retrieval takes the top ten, then removes some.

8
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:30.833
Qdrant checks conditions during the HNSW walk itself.

9
00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:35.667
So filtered queries stay both fast and correct, instead of coming back empty.

10
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:38.033
Here's one query.

11
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:41.767
The embedded question arrives, along with a payload filter.

12
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:44.700
The graph walk applies that filter as it goes.

13
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.033
And out come only valid matches.

14
00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:50.400
Here's a filtered query, simplified.

15
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.333
We connect to a Qdrant server, started with one Docker command.

16
00:00:54.633 --> 00:00:59.820
The filter requires mission Artemis three, and a year of twenty twenty five or later.

17
00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.833
Both are applied during the walk.

18
00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:04.933
Choose it when you need a real server.

19
00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:06.433
Concurrent writers.

20
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:09.300
Multiple services querying the same store.

21
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.800
Or data that must stay on your own infrastructure, without a managed vendor.

22
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.933
Mission Control RAG runs Qdrant in production.

23
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:22.467
It sits in Docker on Kubernetes, behind a FastAPI service.

24
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:27.133
Every query carries filters for mission, date range, and a clearance field.

25
00:01:27.433 --> 00:01:31.054
So contractor accounts never retrieve restricted chunks.

26
00:01:31.054 --> 00:01:34.933
Security enforced inside the index, not in a loop afterward.

27
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:36.913
Let's recap.

28
00:01:36.913 --> 00:01:40.033
Qdrant is a Rust server, from Docker to cluster.

29
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.700
Payload filters run inside the search.

30
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.033
Choose it for self-hosted production.

31
00:01:45.333 --> 00:01:48.267
And access control becomes a payload filter.

32
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.467
Next up: Pinecone.
